.Sh DESCRIPTION
The tinyparse library compiles a grammar, written as S-expression data in a
.Pa .scm
file, into a parser at run time.
The parser turns input bytes into a concrete syntax tree (CST) and a list of
diagnostics.
The library performs no I/O and allocates memory only from the memory grant its
host program gives it: a block of memory and, optionally, a callback that
supplies more.
.Pp
The
.Nm
command is a host program for that library.
It reads grammar and input files, parses, and prints the tree, the
diagnostics, query captures or the node types of a grammar.
It also writes packed grammars, C source generated from a grammar, and
starter projects.
The first argument names the command and the positional arguments follow it;
options come after the positional arguments, in any order.
.Sh GRAMMAR ARGUMENTS
Every command that takes
.Ar GRAMMAR
resolves it in this order:
.Bl -enum
.It
A path to a file named
.Pa package.scm ,
or to a directory that contains
.Pa package.scm ,
names a grammar package.
The command reads the manifest, sets each entry of its
.Li params\&:
as a parameter, and compiles
.Pa grammar.scm
from the same directory.
.It
Otherwise, a path to a readable file names a grammar file.
Relative paths are relative to the working directory.
A directory without
.Pa package.scm
is read as an empty file and fails with
.Ql error: grammar form not found .
.It
Otherwise,
.Ar GRAMMAR
is looked up by name among the standard grammars built into the program,
such as
.Ql std.json ;
.Nm Cm grammars
lists them.
A built-in grammar is used with the parameters of its own manifest.
.It
Otherwise, the command prints
.Ql tinyparse: error: cannot read grammar 'GRAMMAR'
and exits with status 1.
.El
.Pp
A file in the working directory therefore takes the place of a built-in
grammar of the same name.
.Pp
.Cm run ,
.Cm linearize ,
.Cm query
and
.Cm pack
also accept a packed grammar written by
.Cm pack :
a file whose name ends in
.Pa .tpg ,
or whose first four bytes are
.Ql TPG1 ,
is loaded instead of compiled.
.Cm check ,
.Cm node\-types
and
.Cm c
read every file as
.Pa .scm
source; see
.Sx BUGS .
.Pp
A grammar can name other grammars with
.Li (import NAME) ,
.Li (extends NAME)
and
.Li (inject grammar: NAME ...) .
The command resolves
.Ar NAME
by replacing each
.Ql \&.
with
.Ql / ,
appending
.Pa .scm ,
and reading that path relative to the directory of the grammar named on the
command line (for a built-in grammar, the working directory).
.Ql words
resolves to
.Pa DIR/words.scm
and
.Ql lib.words
to
.Pa DIR/lib/words.scm ,
also from inside an imported file.
If no such file exists,
.Ar NAME
is looked up among the built-in standard grammars.
A grammar reached this way is compiled without its manifest.
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
sets a parameter, which the grammar's
.Li (when NAME: ...)
forms test.
It is accepted by every command that compiles a grammar and may be repeated;
for the same
.Ar NAME
the last one wins.
Parameters from a package manifest are set first, so
.Fl \-param
overrides them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-param Ar NAME
sets
.Ar NAME .
.It Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns = No and Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns =0
clear
.Ar NAME .
.It Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns = Ns Ar VALUE
sets
.Ar NAME
for any other
.Ar VALUE ,
including
.Ql false
and
.Ql 00 .
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss check
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Compiles
.Ar GRAMMAR
and prints nothing when it compiles.
When it does not, prints
.Ql error:\~
followed by the fault text to standard error and exits with status 1.
The fault text has no line or column; see
.Sx DIAGNOSTICS .
.Ss run
.Nm
.Cm run
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Fl \-input Ar FILE
.Op Fl \-dump\-tree
.Op Fl \-chunk Ar N
.Op Fl \-trace
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Compiles or loads
.Ar GRAMMAR
and parses the bytes of
.Ar FILE .
Without
.Fl \-dump\-tree
a successful parse prints nothing.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-input Ar FILE
The input to parse.
.Ql \-
reads standard input.
Required.
.It Fl \-dump\-tree
Prints the tree to standard output in the format under
.Sx Tree dump .
.It Fl \-chunk Ar N
Hands the input to the parser
.Ar N
bytes at a time.
The parse runs once the last chunk has arrived, and the tree is the same for
every
.Ar N .
.Ar N
is read as a decimal integer, as
.Xr strtol 3
reads it; when that gives 0 or less, the whole input is handed over at once.
.It Fl \-trace
Prints one line per parser event to standard error, as
.Ql trace: EVENT NAME .
The start rule produces no events.
.Ar EVENT
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li rule.enter
A rule named
.Ar NAME
is tried.
.It Li rule.exit
The rule
.Ar NAME
matched.
A rule that fails produces no
.Li rule.exit .
.It Li token
A token matched and became a leaf.
A literal token's
.Ar NAME
is its text.
Extras produce no event.
.It Li rule.memo\-on
The rule memo switched on while entering
.Ar NAME
(see
.Ev TINYPARSE_RULEMEMO ) .
.It Li rule.replay
The result of
.Ar NAME
at this position was copied from the rule memo instead of parsed again.
.El
.El
.Pp
After the parse,
.Cm run
prints its diagnostics to standard error, after the tree when there is one.
It exits with status 1 when the parse fails or when any diagnostic has error
severity, including a parse that recovered and printed a tree.
A failed parse prints no tree; when it recorded no diagnostics,
.Cm run
prints
.Ql error: parse failed .
A parse that runs out of memory prints
.Ql error: out of memory ,
and any other failure prints
.Ql error: parse failed (result N) ,
where N is the number of the
.Vt tinyparse_result .
.Ss pack
.Nm
.Cm pack
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Fl \-out Ar FILE
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Compiles
.Ar GRAMMAR ,
with its imports and base grammar resolved and its parameters applied, and
writes it to
.Ar FILE
as a packed grammar, conventionally named with
.Pa .tpg .
A packed grammar loads without its
.Pa .scm
sources:
.Cm run ,
.Cm linearize
and
.Cm query
accept it as
.Ar GRAMMAR ,
and a host program loads it with
.Fn tinyparse_load_binary_grammar .
.Fl \-out
is required.
.Ss c
.Nm
.Cm c
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Fl \-out Ar FILE
.Op Fl \-bundle | \-parser | \-parser\-lib
.Op Fl \-name Ar PREFIX
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Compiles
.Ar GRAMMAR
and writes C source to
.Ar FILE .
.Fl \-out
is required.
The mode flag selects what
.Ar FILE
contains; with several,
.Fl \-parser\-lib
wins over
.Fl \-parser ,
and both win over
.Fl \-bundle .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It (no mode flag)
A generated module: the packed grammar as a byte array and the function
.Ft tinyparse_string
.Fn tinyparse_embedded_grammar void ,
which returns it for
.Fn tinyparse_load_binary_grammar .
Parsing runs in the library's VM.
.It Fl \-bundle
The generated module plus a
.Fn main
that parses standard input and prints one node kind per line, indented by
depth, without fields or byte ranges.
On a failed parse it prints each diagnostic's message and exits with status 1.
A recovered parse prints its inserted nodes like any other node, without
.Li MISSING ,
prints no diagnostics and exits with status 0.
.It Fl \-parser
A generated parser: one C function per rule and token, plus a
.Fn main
that parses standard input, prints the tree in the
.Sx Tree dump
format and the diagnostics like
.Cm run ,
and exits with status 1 when the parse fails or any diagnostic has error
severity.
.It Fl \-parser\-lib
The generated parser without
.Fn main .
It defines
.Ft tinyparse_result
.Fn PREFIX_load "tinyparse_engine *" "tinyparse_grammar **"
and
.Ft tinyparse_result
.Fn PREFIX_run "tinyparse_parser *" .
When the start rule's body is
.Li (repeat ITEM) ,
.Li (seq (repeat ITEM))
or
.Li (seq (repeat ITEM) EOF) ,
it also defines
.Ft tinyparse_result
.Fn PREFIX_run_chunk "tinyparse_parser *"
for an input buffer that is still growing.
.It Fl \-name Ar PREFIX
Sets the prefix of every file-scope symbol in a generated parser.
The default prefix is the grammar's name, the symbol after
.Li (grammar ,
so two grammars with different names link into one program without
.Fl \-name .
A module or bundle ignores it.
.El
.Pp
Every mode links against
.Pa libtinyparse
or the amalgamation.
A generated parser
.Pf ( Fl \-parser ,
.Fl \-parser\-lib )
also includes the library's private header
.Pa internal.h ,
which only a source tree provides, so compile it from the
.Pa tinyparse
directory with
.Fl I Ar include Fl I Ar src .
The C API reference describes the generated symbols; see
.Sx SEE ALSO .
.Ss linearize
.Nm
.Cm linearize
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Fl \-input Ar FILE
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Parses
.Ar FILE
and prints the tree as the token stream a query matches against, in the
format under
.Sx Linearized tree .
.Fl \-input
is required;
.Ql \-
reads standard input.
A parse that fails prints its diagnostics and exits with status 1.
A parse that recovers prints the stream, prints no diagnostics and exits with
status 0.
.Ss query
.Nm
.Cm query
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Ar QUERY
.Fl \-input Ar FILE
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Parses
.Ar FILE ,
runs the query in the file
.Ar QUERY
over the tree, and prints one line per capture in the format under
.Sx Query captures .
The query language is described in the query language reference; see
.Sx SEE ALSO .
A query the library rejects prints
.Ql tinyparse: error: invalid query
and exits with status 1, after any captures of the forms before the rejected
one.
Parse failures and recovered parses are handled as by
.Cm linearize .
.Ss node-types
.Nm
.Cm node\-types
.Ar GRAMMAR
.Op Fl \-param Ar NAME Ns Op = Ns Ar VALUE
.No ...
.Pp
Compiles
.Ar GRAMMAR
and prints its node types to standard output as JSON, in the format under
.Sx Node types .
.Ss init
.Nm
.Cm init
.Ar DIR
.Op Fl \-amalgamation | \-lib
.Op Fl \-grammar Ar NAME
.Pp
Creates
.Ar DIR ,
if it does not exist, and writes a starter project into it, replacing files of
the same names:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa grammar.scm
A grammar for a greeting such as
.Ql hello world ,
or with
.Fl \-grammar Ar NAME
the
.Pa grammar.scm
of the built-in standard grammar
.Ar NAME .
.It Pa main.c
A program that reads
.Pa grammar.scm
from the working directory, compiles it, parses standard input and prints one
node kind per line.
It does not install a module resolver, class bindings or parameters, and
gives the engine a fixed 8 MiB block.
.It Pa build.sh
.Ql cc main.c tinyparse.c -o app ,
or with
.Fl \-lib
.Ql cc main.c -ltinyparse -o app .
.It Pa tinyparse.c , Pa tinyparse.h
The amalgamation, copied from the program.
.Fl \-amalgamation
is the default;
.Fl \-lib
writes neither file.
.El
.Pp
Because
.Pa main.c
provides no module resolver and no parameters, a project made with
.Fl \-grammar
builds and parses only for
.Ql std.json
and
.Ql std.shell .
For the other standard grammars
.Pa ./app
exits with status 1 and prints
.Ql error: extends requires a module resolver
.Pf ( Ql std.json5 ,
.Ql std.jsonc ,
.Ql std.typescript ,
.Ql std.tsx ) ,
.Ql error: inject requires a module resolver to find the embedded grammar
.Pf ( Ql std.fence )
or
.Ql error: out of memory reading string
.Pf ( Ql std.javascript ) .
.Pp
.Cm init
rejects any other argument that begins with
.Ql \-
with
.Ql tinyparse: error: init: unknown option 'OPTION' ,
and an unknown
.Fl \-grammar
.Ar NAME
with
.Ql tinyparse: error: unknown grammar 'NAME' ;
both exit with status 1 before writing anything.
.Ss vendor
.Nm
.Cm vendor
.Ar DIR
.Op Fl \-amalgamation | \-lib
.Pp
Creates
.Ar DIR ,
if it does not exist, and writes the amalgamation into it:
.Pa tinyparse.c
and
.Pa tinyparse.h ,
or with
.Fl \-lib
only
.Pa tinyparse.h .
Existing files of the same names are replaced.
Any other argument that begins with
.Ql \-
is rejected with
.Ql tinyparse: error: vendor: unknown option 'OPTION'
and exit status 1, before anything is written.
.Ss grammars
.Nm
.Cm grammars
.Pp
Prints the names of the standard grammars built into the program, one per
line.
.Ss man
.Nm
.Cm man
.Pp
Prints this manual page in mdoc format to standard output.
.Ss version
.Nm
.Cm version ,
.Nm
.Fl \-version
.Pp
Prints
.Ql tinyparse ,
a space and the version, then a newline.
.Ss help
.Nm
.Cm help ,
.Nm
.Fl \-help ,
.Nm
.Fl h ,
.Nm
.Pp
Prints a usage summary to standard output and exits with status 0.
.Sh HOST SERVICES
Before it compiles a grammar, every command gives the library:
.Bl -bullet
.It
a memory grant of 16 MiB whose grow callback takes more memory from
.Xr malloc 3
when a parse needs it;
.It
the module resolver described under
.Sx GRAMMAR ARGUMENTS ;
.It
four character classes for
.Li (class NAME) ,
each matching ASCII bytes only:
.Ql alpha
.Pf ( Ql A\-Z ,
.Ql a\-z ) ,
.Ql digit
.Pf ( Ql 0\-9 ) ,
.Ql alnum
.Pf ( Ql alpha
or
.Ql digit )
and
.Ql space
(space, tab, newline, carriage return).
.El
.Pp
A
.Li (class NAME)
with any other
.Ar NAME
compiles and matches no byte.
.Sh OUTPUT FORMATS
.Ss Tree dump
.Cm run Fl \-dump\-tree
and the
.Fn main
of a
.Fl \-parser
program print the tree in pre-order, one node per line.
This line is a
.Ql close
field holding a
.Ql \&)
token at byte 8:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
      close: ) [8-9] [0,8]-[0,9]
.Ed
.Pp
A line holds, in this order:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Indentation
Two spaces per level below the root.
.It Ql MISSING\~
Present when recovery inserted the node: a zero-width node for input that is
not there.
.It Ql FIELD:\~
Present when the node is bound to a field of its parent.
.It Ar KIND
The name of the rule or token.
A literal token's kind is its text, printed as is, so a literal newline token
ends the line early.
.It Ql \~[LANGUAGE]
Present on every node of an injected region: the name of the grammar that
parsed it, such as
.Ql [std.json] .
.It Ql \~[START-END]
The byte range: 0-based, end-exclusive.
.It Ql \~[ROW,COLUMN]-[ROW,COLUMN]
The start and end points: 0-based rows, and columns counted in bytes from the
start of the row.
.El
.Pp
Trivia and error extras appear in place as nodes of their token kind.
Skipped extras and
.Li EOF
produce no node.
.Ss Linearized tree
.Cm linearize
prints one line per event of a depth-first walk:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li enter Ar KIND
A node with children starts.
.It Li leaf Ar KIND TEXT
A node without children, followed by its bytes as they appear in the input.
The text of a
.Ql MISSING
node is empty.
.It Li exit Ar KIND
The node of the matching
.Li enter
ends.
.El
.Pp
Fields and language tags are not printed.
.Ss Query captures
.Cm query
prints one line per capture, in the order the query reports them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
@NAME START-END TEXT
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar NAME
is the capture name,
.Ar START-END
the captured node's byte range (0-based, end-exclusive), and
.Ar TEXT
the captured bytes, printed as they are, including any newlines.
.Ss Node types
.Cm node\-types
prints a JSON array with one object per rule, hidden rules included, then one
per named token.
Literal tokens are not listed.
Each object has:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li \(dqtype\(dq
The node kind.
.It Li \(dqnamed\(dq
Always
.Li true .
.It Li \(dqfields\(dq
An object with one member per field the rule uses.
Each member is
.Li { \(dqtypes\(dq: [...] } ,
listing
.Li { \(dqtype\(dq: KIND, \(dqnamed\(dq: true }
for every named kind the field can hold; a field that holds only literal
tokens has an empty list.
Tokens have an empty object.
.It Li \(dqchildren\(dq
Present on a rule with named children outside any field:
.Li { \(dqmultiple\(dq: BOOL, \(dqrequired\(dq: false, \(dqtypes\(dq: [...] } .
.Li \(dqmultiple\(dq
is
.Li true
when more than one kind can appear there.
.El
.Pp
The layout follows tree-sitter's
.Pa node-types.json ,
but the meanings differ: tree-sitter's
.Li \(dqmultiple\(dq
says whether more than one child can appear, its
.Li \(dqrequired\(dq
is computed, and its field members carry
.Li \(dqmultiple\(dq
and
.Li \(dqrequired\(dq
and list unnamed types.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev TINYPARSE_RULEMEMO
Controls the rule memo, which records each rule's result at each position so a
backtracking parse does not parse it again.
.Ql always
turns it on from the start of every parse;
.Ql off
turns it off.
Unset or set to any other value, it switches on once a parse has made more
than 32 \(mu L + 65,536 rule calls, where L is the input length in bytes.
Every command that compiles a grammar reads it.
The tree is the same in every mode; the setting exists to test the memo.
.It Ev TINYPARSE_TRACE_LIFECYCLE
When set and not empty,
.Cm run
prints
.Ql lifecycle: STATE
to standard error after the parse, before the tree and the diagnostics.
.Ar STATE
is
.Ql finished
when the parser ran over the whole input, whether or not the input matched,
and
.Ql failed
when a chunk was dropped before the parse.
Other commands ignore it.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa package.scm
The manifest of a grammar package; see
.Sx GRAMMAR ARGUMENTS .
.It Pa grammar.scm
The grammar of a package, in the same directory as
.Pa package.scm .
.It Pa *.tpg
A packed grammar written by
.Cm pack .
Its first four bytes are
.Ql TPG1 .
.It Pa DIR/grammar.scm , DIR/main.c , DIR/build.sh , DIR/tinyparse.c , DIR/tinyparse.h
The files
.Cm init
and
.Cm vendor
write.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success, including
.Cm help ,
.Cm version ,
.Cm man
and
.Cm grammars ,
and a
.Cm linearize
or
.Cm query
whose parse recovered.
.It 1
A grammar that does not compile, a parse that fails, a
.Cm run
with any error-severity diagnostic, an unreadable file, a missing required
option, a rejected query, an unknown option for
.Cm init
or
.Cm vendor ,
an unknown
.Fl \-grammar
name for
.Cm init ,
and running out of memory.
.It 2
An unknown command.
The usage summary goes to standard error.
.El
.Pp
Commands other than
.Cm init
and
.Cm vendor
ignore options they do not know and extra arguments.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Parse JSON with the built-in
.Ql std.json
grammar:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf '{"a": [1, 2]}' > in.json
tinyparse run std.json --input in.json --dump-tree
.Ed
.Pp
Parse standard input in 1-byte chunks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf '(a (b c))' | tinyparse run examples/sexpr/sexpr.scm \e
    --input - --chunk 1 --dump-tree
.Ed
.Pp
Pack a grammar once and parse with the packed file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinyparse pack std.json --out json.tpg
tinyparse run json.tpg --input in.json --dump-tree
.Ed
.Pp
Print the first item of every list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf '(a (b c))' > input.txt
printf '%s\en' '(List open: (_) . (Form) @first)' > first.scm
tinyparse query examples/sexpr/sexpr.scm first.scm --input input.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a parser library with the prefix
.Ql sexpr
and compile it from the
.Pa tinyparse
directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinyparse c examples/sexpr/sexpr.scm --out sexpr_parser.c --parser-lib
cc -std=c99 -I include -I src -c sexpr_parser.c
.Ed
.Pp
Start a project that builds with one C compiler:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinyparse init hello
cd hello && sh build.sh && echo 'hello world' | ./app
.Ed
.Pp
Read this page in a terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tinyparse man | mandoc -a
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Errors from the command itself are printed as
.Ql tinyparse: error: MESSAGE .
A grammar that fails to compile or load prints
.Ql error: FAULT ,
the text the library reports, with no position.
A parse prints each diagnostic as
.Bd -literal -offset indent
MESSAGE [CODE]
  help: HELP
.Ed
.Pp
The help line appears only when the diagnostic has help text.
The library's own messages begin with
.Ql error:\~ ,
as in
.Ql error: unexpected input [parse.syntax] ;
a message from a grammar's
.Li recover
form is printed as the grammar wrote it.
Labels, notes and positions are not printed.
Diagnostics of an injected region are not reported.
The codes and fields are listed in the diagnostics reference; see
.Sx SEE ALSO .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cc 1 ,
.Xr mandoc 1
.Pp
The references in the
.Pa tinyparse
directory of the source repository,
.Lk https://github.com/dhamidi/tinyactors :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa docs/reference/grammar.md
the grammar language and
.Pa package.scm
.It Pa docs/reference/query-language.md
the query language
.It Pa docs/reference/diagnostics.md
diagnostic codes and faults
.It Pa docs/reference/c-api.md
the C API and the symbols of generated C
.It Pa docs/reference/std-grammars.md
the standard grammars
.El
